# Global Engineering Governance (1.0)

Applies to all Cursor work in this environment. **Not a product requirements doc.**

If a repo has more specific `AGENTS.md` / `.cursor/rules`, prefer those — but **never** trade away security or correctness for speed.

Conflict order: platform/system → security → project/business rules → this global rule → general preferences.

## Priorities

Security > correctness > data integrity > maintainability > performance > UX > speed.  
Prefer simple, maintainable solutions. No unnecessary complexity or rewrites.

## Existing project first

Before changing anything: **INSPECT** structure, lockfile, architecture, tests, env, deploy, DB, existing rules/skills/MCP.  
Do not assume the project is empty or that existing architecture/rules/deps are wrong. Do not break working code without a justified reason.

## Git & secrets

- No force push, unrelated resets, history rewrites, or secret/`.env` commits without explicit approval
- Meaningful, focused commits; inspect diff; run relevant lint/tests before commit
- Keep `.env.example` with **names only** (no real values). When reporting env: **name + configured/not** — never values
- Never put server secrets in client code (if unsure, treat as secret)

## Accounts & external services

Never assume the currently logged-in GitHub/Supabase/Vercel/cloud account is correct.  
Before creating or changing resources: verify account / org / project / resource against this repo’s config. If ambiguous → **STOP**. No duplicate projects, no silent deletes.

**Account map (reference only — never hard-code emails in app code):** run `install.sh` (prompts for GitHub/Supabase/Vercel identity) or edit `account-map.mdc` next to this rule. Project map overrides user-global map. Do not edit this governance rule just to change accounts.

Multi-project: do not reuse the previous chat’s GitHub/Supabase/Vercel identity for a different repo.

## Multi-tenant & APIs

Tenant isolation is **server-side**. Never trust client org/tenant/role/ownership.  
Auth ≠ authorization — verify authorization on every protected action.  
Validate input against SQLi, XSS, CSRF, SSRF, IDOR, mass assignment, path traversal, unsafe redirects.

Uploads are untrusted: size, MIME, extension, auth, path. Do not make private storage public for convenience; use signed URLs when needed.

Do not send passwords, tokens, or unnecessary PII to AI providers. Keep tenant boundaries. Important data changes need validation/confirmation.

## DB & deploy

Migrations only; no prod reset/drop/truncate without explicit approval.  
Before deploy: repo, branch, target account/project, env, migrations, build, tests. Never deploy to an unknown project.

## Quality

Do not silently swallow errors. User errors must not leak secrets/stacks. Never log secrets.  
Before claiming meaningful work done: lint / typecheck / test / build as applicable. Do not fake green CI by skipping tests.

## CI · deploy alerts

Do not leave GitHub Actions / host deploy failures ignored. Prefer fixing root cause over muting alerts. Do not skip tests or disable gates to fake green.

Verify GitHub account ≠ hosting account when they differ. Connect auto-deploy only after required env (including secrets) is set.

## When uncertain

INSPECT. Security → VERIFY. Destructive → STOP. Routine → PROCEED. Failure → fix root cause. Working solution → improve, don’t rewrite. Project rule exists → follow it unless it violates security/higher rules.
